{
  "image_name": "749210e2faccead9d76b39ce4c652951.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a dry-electrode manufacturing route for battery films. On the left, the electrode ingredients—active material, conductive agent, and binder—are first combined by dry mixing, either in a ball mill or a double-blade blender. The mixed powder appears to form composite agglomerates in which dark conductive particles are distributed around larger blue active-material particles, with binder helping hold the mixture together.\n\nThe right side shows several ways this dry mixture can be turned into a continuous electrode layer on a moving substrate. In a “Maxwell type” process, heating and mechanical action cause binder fibrillization, producing a cohesive film that is then compacted by calendering. A related route uses hot pressing or melt extrusion before the film is similarly transferred and densified. A third approach sprays the dry material onto the substrate and then applies hot calendering.\n\nTaken together, the image highlights a solvent-free alternative to conventional wet coating: powders are mixed, structured into a self-supporting or deposited layer, and compressed into an electrode sheet. The main comparison is between different processing methods that share the same dry-mixing starting point and end with calendered electrode films.",
